; Nolang doc catalog: en
; Source-comment translations, keyed by the anchor an author wrote in a
; trailing `[label](#anchor)` line. This file holds TRANSLATIONS ONLY: an
; anchor that is absent falls back to the comment text in the .no source, so
; untranslated blocks degrade to the author's language instead of shipping a
; copy of it here. Every key must still exist in some .no source, or the gate
; rejects it as an orphan.
{
  'args.count': `
number of arguments, including the program name
delegates to the os.args() builtin, which returns the whole argv vector; the length of that vector is the count
`
  'args.get': `
the i-th argument
delegates to the os.arg(i) builtin, which reads argv[i] of main; an out-of-range i returns nil
`
  'args.get-option': `
reads an option value (e.g. --output=xxx)
returns val ?str: ok = the option value, nil = not found
`
  'args.get-positional': `
reads the i-th positional argument, skipping flags
returns val ?str: ok = the positional value, nil = absent
`
  'args.has-flag': 'reports whether a flag is present (e.g. --verbose, -v)'
  'args.program': 'the program name (argument 0)'
  'arr.arr-stack-init': `
[n]t.arr-stack-init: initialises the stack state
cap is set to the array size n automatically
returns s arr-stack: the initialised stack state
`
  'arr.arr-stack-peek': `
[n]t.arr-stack-peek: looks at the top element without removing it
s arr-stack: the stack state
returns val ?t: ok = the top value, nil = the stack is empty
`
  'arr.arr-stack-pop': `
[n]t.arr-stack-pop: pops the element at the top of the stack
s arr-stack: the stack state
returns val ?t: ok = the popped value, nil = the stack is empty
`
  'arr.arr-stack-push': `
[n]t.arr-stack-push: pushes an element onto the top of the stack
s arr-stack: the stack state
val t: the value to push
returns ok bool: true = success, false = the stack is full
`
  'arr.asc': '[n]t.asc: sorts ascending in place (insertion sort)'
  'arr.at': `
[n]t.at: safe indexed access
i i64: the index
returns val ?t: nil when out of range
`
  'arr.clone': '[n]t.clone: copies the array into dst'
  'arr.contains': '[n]t.contains: reports whether the value val is present'
  'arr.count': `
[n]t.count: counts the occurrences of a value
val t: the value to count
returns cnt i64: the number of occurrences
`
  'arr.desc': '[n]t.desc: sorts descending in place (insertion sort)'
  'arr.eq': '[n]t.eq: compares two arrays for equality'
  'arr.fill': '[n]t.fill: fills everything with val'
  'arr.first': '[n]t.first: the first element'
  'arr.index-of': '[n]t.index-of: returns the index of a value, or -1'
  'arr.last': '[n]t.last: the last element'
  'arr.len': `
[n]t.len: returns the array length
Note: the output parameter is named out rather than n so it does not collide with the generic parameter n
`
  'arr.link-clear': `
[n]t.link-clear: empties the linked list and reclaims every node
l link: the linked-list state
`
  'arr.link-find': `
[n]t.link-find: finds the position of a value
l link: the linked-list state
val t: the value to find
returns pos i64: the position index, -1 = not found
`
  'arr.link-get-at': `
[n]t.link-get-at: gets the element at a position
l link: the linked-list state
pos i64: the position
returns val ?t: ok = the element value, nil = the position is out of range
`
  'arr.link-init': `
[n]t.link-init: initialises a linked list
nxt []i64: the next-node index array (caller-supplied, size >= n)
prv []i64: the previous-node index array (caller-supplied, size >= n)
returns l link: the initialised linked list
`
  'arr.link-insert-at': `
[n]t.link-insert-at: inserts an element at a position
l link: the linked-list state
pos i64: the insertion position (0 = head, size = tail)
val t: the value to insert
returns ok bool: true = success, false = the position is out of range or the node pool is full
`
  'arr.link-peek-back': `
[n]t.link-peek-back: looks at the tail element without removing it
l link: the linked-list state
returns val ?t: ok = the tail value, nil = the list is empty
`
  'arr.link-peek-front': `
[n]t.link-peek-front: looks at the head element without removing it
l link: the linked-list state
returns val ?t: ok = the head value, nil = the list is empty
`
  'arr.link-pop-back': `
[n]t.link-pop-back: pops the element at the tail of the linked list
l link: the linked-list state
returns val ?t: ok = the popped value, nil = the list is empty
`
  'arr.link-pop-front': `
[n]t.link-pop-front: pops the element at the head of the linked list
l link: the linked-list state
returns val ?t: ok = the popped value, nil = the list is empty
`
  'arr.link-push-back': `
[n]t.link-push-back: inserts an element at the tail of the linked list
l link: the linked-list state
val t: the value to insert
returns ok bool: true = success, false = the node pool is full
`
  'arr.link-push-front': `
[n]t.link-push-front: inserts an element at the head of the linked list
l link: the linked-list state
val t: the value to insert
returns ok bool: true = success, false = the node pool is full
`
  'arr.link-remove-at': `
[n]t.link-remove-at: removes the element at a position
l link: the linked-list state
pos i64: the position to remove
returns val ?t: ok = the removed value, nil = the position is out of range
`
  'arr.link-reverse': `
[n]t.link-reverse: reverses the linked list
Swaps every node's next/prev, then swaps head/tail
