  'async.cancel': `
⚠️ deprecated: a hand-rolled cancel is deprecated; use a coroutine group (a bare block { ... }) instead.
cancel: cancels a background task started by run()
task is the opaque handle run() returned (i8*). Returns void.
`
  'async.cancelled': `
⚠️ deprecated: a hand-rolled cancelled is deprecated; use a coroutine group (a bare block { ... }) instead.
cancelled: reports whether the async task currently executing has been cancelled
returns bool. Only meaningful inside an async task (an -async function scheduled by the event loop).
`
  'bigint._add': `
a + b (operator overload; written ?bigint, which the compiler treats as bigint)
Route C: a union parameter `(b big)` plus a type-destructuring match. The bigint arm delegates
to `add-bigint`, the i64 arm to `_add-i64`. The core is deliberately not inlined into the type
arm: measured behaviour is that inside a match-arm BlockStatement, mutating the named output
parameter `c` as `c = expr` and then `c.sign = ...` does not write back (the sign is lost, so
-100+7 wrongly yields 93). Delegating to a plain method restores the `c.sign` mutation
semantics, for the same reason _mul delegates to mul-bigint.
`
  'bigint._add-i64': 'c = self + v (small-integer addition)'
  'bigint._div': `
a / b (operator overload; division by zero returns zero, following div-mod; the route-C
i64 arm goes through _div-i64)
`
  'bigint._div-i64': 'q = self / v (small-integer division)'
  'bigint._mod': `
a % b (operator overload; division by zero returns zero, following div-mod; the route-C
i64 arm goes through _mod-i64, whose result is ?i64. That result is unwrapped first (failure
yields 0, matching div-mod's zero on division by zero) and then promoted with from-i64 to
bigint so that it matches the output parameter r).
`
  'bigint._mod-i64': `
r = self % v (small-integer remainder)
Returns ?i64: under the D6 unified option rule `cur % r-v` already is ?i64 (it fails when the
divisor is 0). Deliberately declaring the result parameter as plain in order to flatten it
would cause a silent unwrap, so it is declared ?i64 as-is.
`
  'bigint._mul': `
a * b (operator overload; route C is the same as _add, both arms delegate to a concrete
method)
The bigint arm delegates to mul-bigint, the i64 arm to _mul-i64. The core is not inlined into
the type arm because a heavyweight loop / `with-len` / `#{overflow=wrap}` embedded in a
match-arm BlockStatement crashes the lowering boundary (measured: segfault). Delegating to a
plain method restores the `.` receiver and output-parameter semantics, matching the working
i64 arm of _add.
`
  'bigint._mul-i64': 'self * v (small-integer multiplication)'
  'bigint._sub': 'a - b (operator overload; delegation strategy is the same as _add)'
  'bigint.abs-add': '|self| + |b| (signs ignored)'
  'bigint.abs-cmp': 'Compares |self| with |b| (1 = greater, 0 = equal, -1 = smaller)'
  'bigint.abs-sub': '|self| - |b| (assumes |self| >= |b|, signs ignored)'
  'bigint.add-bigint': `
The big-plus-big core of self + b (a plain concrete method, not an operator overload; _add
delegates here)
Arguments are named: the s in `v.abs-sub(s)` must first be captured with `s = .` (a bare `.`
as a method argument hits a known lowering gap).
`
  'bigint.cmp': 'Compares self with b (1 = greater, 0 = equal, -1 = smaller)'
  'bigint.copy': `
Deep-copies a bigint
Warning: not deprecated (unlike str/txt.copy). The assignment `b = a` only copies the
{sign,limbs,len} descriptor and shares the same limbs buffer with a (measured: after
a.limbs[0] = 99, b.limbs[0] is also 99), so it has no deep-copy semantics. Use this method
whenever you need an independent snapshot.
`
  'bigint.div-mod': 'self / b, returning both the quotient and the remainder'
  'bigint.eq': 'Is self == b?'
  'bigint.from-i64': 'Builds a bigint from an i64'
  'bigint.from-u64': 'Builds a bigint from an unsigned i64 (always positive)'
  'bigint.gcd': 'g = gcd(self, b) (Euclidean algorithm)'
  'bigint.is-neg': 'Whether the value is negative'
  'bigint.is-pos': 'Whether the value is positive'
  'bigint.is-zero': 'Whether the value is zero'
  'bigint.lcm': 'l = lcm(self, b)'
  'bigint.mod-pow': 'r = self^exp mod modulus'
  'bigint.mul-bigint': `
The big-integer core of |self| * |b| (a plain concrete method, not an operator overload;
_mul delegates here)
`
  'bigint.normalize': 'Strips leading zeros and guarantees len >= 1'
  'bigint.one': 'Builds a bigint holding one'
  'bigint.pow': 'c = self^n (n >= 0, a non-negative integer power)'
  'bigint.shl': 'c = a << n'
  'bigint.shl1': 'self = self << 1'
  'bigint.shr': 'c = a >> n'
  'bigint.shr1': 'self = self >> 1'
  'bigint.sub-bigint': 'The big-minus-big core of self - b (a plain concrete method; _sub delegates here)'
  'bigint.to-hex': `
Formats a bigint as a lowercase hexadecimal string
Returns out str: the hexadecimal string
`
  'bigint.to-i64': `
Why this exists: small-integer fast paths such as `_mod-i64` / `_div-i64` are private to this
module (see the privacy convention above). Outside modules receive a bigint produced by an
operator (`% / * + -`, whose range is naturally bounded by the dividend within i64); to get an
i64 back they must use this public exit rather than reaching for a private method.
`
  'bigint.to-str': `
Formats a bigint as a decimal string
Returns out str: the decimal string
`
  'bigint.zero': 'Builds a zero bigint'
  'bool.to-str': 'converts a bool to the string "true" or "false"'
  'bufio.reader.close': 'closes the reader'
  'bufio.reader.fill': 'refills the buffer'
  'bufio.reader.init': 'creates a buffered reader'
