reads one byte
returns b ?byte: ok = the byte value, nil = EOF or error
`
  'bufio.reader.read-line': `
reads one line
returns line ?str: ok = the line, nil = EOF or error
`
  'bytes.index': `
[]byte.index: searches a []byte for the substring target
Returns the position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
`
  'bytes.index-from': `
[]byte.index-from: searches a []byte for the substring target starting at from
Returns the position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
`
  'bytes.len': `
[]byte.len: returns the length of the byte slice
byte is a distinct type rather than an alias of u8, so []byte.len is not instantiated automatically from []t.len.
`
  'bytes.slice': `
[]byte.slice: takes the sub-slice [start, end)
Returns a new slice
`
  'bytes.to-bytes-str': `
[]byte.to-bytes-str: a 1:1 byte-by-byte copy (keeping the original []byte.to-str semantics).
Used when buf already is a valid UTF-8 byte stream (a file read, a subprocess's output) and no per-code-point re-encoding is wanted.
This function is equivalent to the old []byte.to-str.
`
  'bytes.to-chars': `
⚠️ compiler limitation: neither the legacy nor the MIR backend supports []char (i32 element) slices completely
(unlike []byte / []i64, []char's len/cap/stride have latent codegen errors, and a constructed []char corrupts memory out of bounds at run time), so this method is not usable yet.
If all you need is the end result of byte -> code point -> UTF-8 string, just use b.to-str() —
[]byte.to-str already inlines that semantics (each byte re-encoded as a 0..255 code point) and needs no []char.
`
  'bytes.to-hex': '[]byte.to-hex: encodes a []byte as an uppercase hexadecimal string'
  'bytes.to-hex-lower': '[]byte.to-hex-lower: encodes a []byte as a lowercase hexadecimal string'
  'bytes.to-i64-be': '[]byte.to-i64-be: converts big-endian bytes to an int64 (1 to 8 bytes)'
  'bytes.to-i64-le': '[]byte.to-i64-le: converts little-endian bytes to an int64 (1 to 8 bytes)'
  'bytes.to-str': `
⚠️ semantics change (2026-09-06, at the requester's request): this method used to be a 1:1 byte-by-byte copy, the contract buf.to-str() relies on (reading a file or a subprocess's output, where buf already is a valid UTF-8 byte stream).
With the switch to byte -> code point -> UTF-8 re-encoding, a valid multi-byte UTF-8 stream gets encoded twice and corrupted
(for example 0xC3 0xA9 read from disk is treated as code points 0xC3 and 0xA9 and re-encoded separately). If you need a verbatim copy of a
valid UTF-8 byte stream (file or subprocess reads), use b.to-bytes-str() (see below), which keeps the 1:1 copy semantics, or work with the underlying slice directly.
`
  'bytes.to-txt': `
[]byte.to-txt: converts a []byte to a txt (truncated to 255 bytes)
returns out txt: a fixed 256-byte txt structure
`
  'char.is-alnum': 'reports whether the character is a letter or a digit'
  'char.is-alpha': `
reports whether the character is a letter (A-Z a-z)
an alias of is-letter
`
  'char.is-digit': 'reports whether the character is a digit (0-9)'
  'char.is-letter': 'reports whether the character is a letter (A-Z a-z)'
  'char.is-lower': 'reports whether the character is a lowercase letter (a-z)'
  'char.is-space': 'reports whether the character is whitespace (space, tab, newline, cr)'
  'char.is-upper': 'reports whether the character is an uppercase letter (A-Z)'
  'char.to-bytes': 'converts to the UTF-8 encoded byte array'
  'char.to-lower': 'converts to lower case (ASCII only)'
  'char.to-str': 'converts to the UTF-8 encoded string'
  'char.to-txt': `
converts to a UTF-8 encoded txt (the fixed 256-byte structure)
Reuses char.to-bytes to obtain the 1 to 4 byte UTF-8 sequence, then copies it into the txt buffer byte by byte.
Consistent with str.to-txt / []byte.to-txt it returns a plain txt (converting a single code point cannot fail, so no ?txt).
With it, txt.to-upper / to-lower and friends can be rewritten as "take a char -> char.to-lower -> char.to-txt",
no longer depending on indexed writes through txt[i], which returns a char.
`
  'char.to-upper': 'converts to upper case (ASCII only)'
  'chars.to-str': `
⚠️ note: as of 2026-09-06 nolang's support for the []char slice type is incomplete, so this method is not usable yet:
- legacy backend: with-cap/push on []char disagree about the unit of len/cap (elements versus bytes), so .len() reports too many elements and []char.to-str reads past the end and produces garbage (a single push(72) was measured emitting "H" plus garbage). []char literals and indexed reads do use the correct i32 stride, but push corrupts the slice's len field.
- MIR=3 gate: the compiler supports neither the []char element type nor the new []t.to-chars generic method and reports "unknown callee []t.to-chars" outright, so nothing compiles.
Do not rely on to-chars / []char.to-str until the compiler's []char slice support is fixed.
`
