  'global.exit': `
terminates the process (ends it with status code c)
exit(0)   -> normal exit
exit(1)   -> abnormal exit
`
  'global.format': `
formats a string with {name:spec} named fields and returns it (replaces sprintf)
s = format('{x:.2f}')          -> '3.14'
s = format('{name} is {age}')  -> 'Alice is 30'
⚠️ accepts a single format string only (there is no multi-argument form)
`
  'global.print': `
writes a line to standard output, with {name:spec} named format fields
print('hello {name}')       -> hello world (when name = 'world')
print('{x:.2f}')            -> 3.14 (when x = 3.14159)
multiple arguments: print(a, b, c) joins them with spaces and emits a single newline at the end of the line.
With several arguments each string literal is its own template (see "with multiple arguments, literals are templates" above):
print('result={val}', 42, 'result={val}')  -> result=42 42 result=42
`
  'global.with-cap': `
creates a string or slice with the given capacity (len=0); the type comes from the left-hand side of the assignment
s str = with-cap(256)       -> a str pre-allocating 256 bytes
v []i64 = with-cap(100)     -> a slice pre-allocating 100 elements
`
  'global.with-cap-len': `
creates a string or slice with the given capacity and length; the type comes from the left-hand side of the assignment
v []i64 = with-cap-len(200, 100)  -> a slice with capacity 200 and length 100
`
  'global.with-len': `
creates a string or slice with the given length; the type comes from the left-hand side of the assignment
s str = with-len(10)        -> a str of length 10
v []i64 = with-len(100)     -> a slice of length 100
`
