  'json.is-num': 'reports whether it is a number'
  'json.is-obj': 'reports whether it is an object'
  'json.is-str': 'reports whether it is a string'
  'json.json-clone-tree': `
Why the deep copy is required: json.pool is an #{inline=false} pointer field, and the compiler's pointer-field
copying has no borrow semantics yet (see the copy-tree note and the hybrid-ownership discussion in src/mir),
so a plain `child = json { pool: .pool, ... }` leaves parent and child with separate vec headers over one shared
data buffer; as soon as either handle triggers a vec realloc, the other handle's vec header points at the old,
released buffer -> a dangling read (stringify returns null / data comes out scrambled). With an independent deep copy,
each side's alloc and growth write to its own buffer and neither disturbs the other.
`
  'json.json-pool.add-child': `
attaches the child node child-idx (key name key; pass the empty string for arrays) to node node-idx
Implements the variable-length child list as a linked edge table, so no node has a fixed child limit
`
  'json.json-pool.alloc': 'allocates a new node and returns its index (the heap vec grows automatically, so the pool is no longer bounded)'
  'json.json-pool.arr-get': `
gets the child node at an index of an array node
returns val-idx = the child node index, ok = success
`
  'json.json-pool.arr-len': 'gets the array length'
  'json.json-pool.copy-tree': `
Pool identity cannot rest on `src.id == .id`: a module-level binding cannot be rebound (see the JSON-POOL-NEXT-ID note),
so every pool's id is always 1, and this test used to mistake every cross-pool copy for a same-pool one, leaving the root of the
subtree json.arr-get / json.get returned permanently at -1. It is now guaranteed at the call sites instead:
- json-clone-tree: the receiver is a brand-new pool straight out of init;
- json.set-key: val is first copied into a temporary new pool (json-clone-tree) and then copied across into the receiver,
so both ends of either copy are necessarily different pools, and a self-reference is safe too.
`
  'json.json-pool.get-bool': `
gets the boolean value
returns val = the boolean, ok = success
`
  'json.json-pool.get-key': `
gets the index of the child node for a key of an object node
returns val-idx = the child node index, ok = found
`
  'json.json-pool.get-kind': 'gets the node kind'
  'json.json-pool.get-num': 'gets the numeric value'
  'json.json-pool.get-str': `
gets the string value
returns out = a copy of the string, ok = success
`
