  'number.f64.sin': 'Trigonometric functions (radians; f64/f32 methods)'
  'number.f64.sinh': 'Hyperbolic functions (f64/f32 methods)'
  'number.f64.sqrt': 'Powers and roots (f64/f32 methods)'
  'number.f64.tan': 'tangent (libm tan, equivalent to sin/cos)'
  'number.f64.to-bits': `
extracts the 64-bit IEEE-754 bit pattern of an f64 as a u64 (bitcast double->i64).
Registered as ForwardFunc "f64-to-bits" by src/builtin/bits.go, implemented as a native LLVM bitcast and independent of libm.
f64.to-str takes the bit pattern first, then splits sign/exp/frac, before expanding the shortest round-trip form.
`
  'number.f64.to-f32': 'narrows an f64 to f32 (may lose precision)'
  'number.f64.to-i32': 'float to 32-bit signed integer (saturating)'
  'number.f64.to-i64': 'float to integer (truncating)'
  'number.f64.to-str': `
converts an f64 to a string (a direct Nolang implementation, **shortest round-trip** decimal, matching Go's %v)
It takes the fewest significant digits that reproduce the double exactly (IEEE-754 field decomposition plus bigint exact arithmetic) and keeps %g's notation choice: scientific notation when E <= -5 or E >= 6, fixed point otherwise.
Special values match Go: NaN -> "NaN", +Inf -> "+Inf", -Inf -> "-Inf", ±0 -> "0".
The backend has no native f64 formatter (the sprintf CLibCall was removed), so the whole routine is inlined in the method body.
`
  'number.f64.to-str-fixed': `
fixed-point conversion
precision i64: number of fractional digits (>= 0; keep it <= 17 to avoid i64 overflow)
returns out str: the string form (e.g. "3.14", "-0.005", "100")
`
  'number.f64.to-str-general': `
general format (chooses fixed point or scientific notation automatically)
precision i64: maximum number of significant digits (>= 1)
returns out str: the string form (trailing zeros stripped automatically)
`
  'number.f64.to-str-sci': `
scientific notation
precision i64: number of fractional digits (>= 0)
returns out str: the string form (e.g. "3.14e+2", "-5.0e-3")
`
  'number.f64.to-u32': 'float to 32-bit unsigned integer (saturating)'
  'number.f64.to-u64': 'float to 64-bit unsigned integer (saturating)'
  'number.i128.sqrt': `
── 128-bit integer method copies (i128 / u128) ──
128-bit arithmetic itself works (operands travel the LLVM i128 lane), but two things must be handled together:
(1) Local variables need explicit types (`n i128 = .`, `lo i128 = 1`, ...). Untyped, the checker infers i64 for intermediates mixed with literals, so the loop converges on a wrong value or hangs outright (measured with a binary search on i128.MAX). An untyped u128 also trips an i64->u128 assignment error.
(2) Overflow-annotation layout: i128 is signed and division can overflow on INT_MIN/-1, so every arithmetic statement gets `#{overflow=wrap}`; u128 is unsigned, so ÷/% cannot overflow and the annotation goes only on + − and on divisions by a literal — not on variable-to-variable ÷/%, which would raise an overflow-ineffective warning.
The annotation must sit on its own line immediately before the target statement (a guard chained onto a single `;` line makes the 128-bit codegen drop the whole main body).
This also fixes codegen's isUnsignedRaw omitting u128: comparisons and divisions on u128 used to go through the signed predicate, misordering values in u128.MAX's range (>= 2^127); after the fix u128.sqrt(u128.MAX)=2^64-1 exactly.
`
  'number.i128.to-f64': '128-bit signed integer to float'
  'number.i16.sqrt': 'i16'
  'number.i16.to-str': 'converts an i16 to a string (widened to i64 and reused)'
  'number.i32.sqrt': 'i32'
  'number.i32.to-str': 'converts an i32 to a string (implicitly widened to i64, then reusing the native i64.to-str path)'
  'number.i64.is-prime': `
primality test — an i64 method
Trial division, O(√n): skip even numbers first, then try odd divisors; the loop stops at d <= n/d so d*d cannot overflow, and the divisor is always >= 1 so it cannot trap (it also avoids an int union receiver, see above)
`
  'number.i64.sqrt': `
integer square root floor(√n) — an i64 method
n < 0 has no solution in the integers and returns 0; binary search, O(log n), comparing with n/mid rather than mid*mid so nothing overflows (same wrap-annotation convention as gcd/even in math.no).
It does not use an int union receiver (which would work for every width): the int alias is defined in std/int.no, and a cross-module `int.X` method gets double-prefixed by prefixMethodNames into `int.int.X`, which no monomorphised call site matches, producing an unknown-callee error.
`
  'number.i64.to-bytes-be': `
converts an int64 to 8 big-endian bytes
usage:
v = 0x1234
out = v.to-bytes-be()   // out = [0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x12,0x34]
`
  'number.i64.to-bytes-le': `
converts an int64 to 8 little-endian bytes
usage:
v = 0x1234
out = v.to-bytes-le()   // out = [0x34,0x12,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00]
`
  'number.i64.to-f64': 'integer to float'
  'number.i64.to-i32': 'narrows the integer width (truncating to the low 32 bits)'
  'number.i64.to-str': `
converts an i64 to a string
#{buildin}: goes through the native @str_from_i64 (src/builtin/strconv.go registers ForwardFunc "i64-to-str", and codegen matches that name onto the native inline path). i64 has a native formatter, so it is a direct builtin with no Nolang loop wrapped around it.
`
  'number.i8.sqrt': 'i8 (signed: keeps the negative guard)'
  'number.i8.to-str': 'converts an i8 to a string (widened to i64 and reused)'
  'number.pow': `
integer power base^n (n >= 0, i64) — called as number.pow (module prefix).
Same name as math.no's f64 pow but a different module, so the build adds a number. prefix;
synonymous with math.pow-int (the int generic fast power), but with an explicit i64 signature so that
assertions such as number.pow(2, 10) in test/std/number.no hold.
`
  'number.u128.to-f64': '128-bit unsigned integer to float'
  'number.u16.sqrt': 'u16'
  'number.u16.to-str': 'converts a u16 to a string (widened to u64 and reused)'
  'number.u32.sqrt': 'u32'
