  'str._str-dig-val': 'converts a single digit character (0-15) to its value; an invalid character returns -1'
  'str.at': `
[deprecated] str.at: character (code point) index read — use s[idx] instead (native indexing, returns ?char)
str.at: safe indexed access (by character index; for a byte offset use s.byte(i))
i i64: the character (code point) index
returns val ?byte: the code point value of that character (truncated when it exceeds the byte range); nil when out of range
`
  'str.bigint-bitlen': 'returns the bit length of a positive bigint (floor(log2)+1)'
  'str.byte': `
returns the raw byte value at BYTE offset i (a raw byte read)
Unlike s[i]: s[i] indexes by code point, whereas this method takes a byte offset into the underlying UTF-8 buffer.
Returns ?byte: nil when out of range (i < 0 or i >= len-bytes()), matching the safe-indexing semantics of arr[i]; the caller must handle the option (match / ?= / #{index-out = DEF}).
Handled by the compiler as a builtin (#{buildin}): codegen intercepts it and expands a bounds check plus GEP+load, so there is no std function body. The signature is also registered in src/builtin/str.go's BuiltinMethodList (MethodName "byte", Intercepted), and the bare-name fallback covers both str and txt.
`
  'str.byte-index': `
converts a code point (character) index to a byte offset
Returns the byte position where the cp-pos-th code point starts; when cp-pos is past the end, returns .len
`
  'str.compare': `
compares two strings lexicographically
returns -1 (a<b), 0 (equal), 1 (a>b)
`
  'str.contains': `
reports whether s contains the substring target
returns true = contains, false = does not
`
  'str.copy': `
copies src into dst (the whole string)
⚠️ DEPRECATED: assignment `dst = src` already has copy semantics — when src is read again later, the ownership analysis rewrites the assignment into a deep copy (OpClone), so a manual copy is unnecessary. Kept for compatibility only.
`
  'str.count': `
counts the chars (code points) in a string
.len already gives the byte count, so count provides the code point count
`
  'str.cp-index': `
converts a byte offset to a code point (character) index
Returns the number of code points within s[0..byte-pos)
`
  'str.decode-cp': `
decodes one UTF-8 code point starting at position i
returns cp = the code point value, clen = the sequence length in bytes (1-4)
`
  'str.empty': `
reports whether the string is empty
returns true = empty, false = not empty
`
  'str.ends-with': `
reports whether s ends with target
returns true = yes, false = no
`
  'str.eq-full': `
compares two strings for exact equality
b str: the other string
returns yes bool: true = equal, false = not equal
`
  'str.eq-raw': `
str.eq: compares the first n bytes of two strings
returns true = equal, false = not equal
`
  'str.fields': `
splits the string on one or more consecutive whitespace characters
Whitespace: space (32), tab (9), newline (10), carriage return (13)
returns out []str: the non-empty substrings
Consecutive whitespace counts as a single separator, and leading or trailing whitespace produces no empty element
`
  'str.fill': 'fills the whole string with a byte value'
  'str.find-byte-from': `
str.index: searches s for the substring target
Uses the Sunday algorithm, O(n/m) on average and O(n*m) in the worst case
Returns the position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
str.find-byte-from: the internal byte-level search (Sunday algorithm)
Returns the BYTE offset of the first occurrence of target, or -1 when absent
from-byte is the starting byte position; used internally when slicing by bytes, as split does
`
  'str.from-hex': `
decodes a hexadecimal string into []byte
returns Option:
ok(bytes) - success
nil        - empty string
err('msg') - contains an invalid hexadecimal character
`
  'str.index': `
searches s for the substring target
Uses the Sunday algorithm, O(n/m) on average and O(n*m) in the worst case
Returns the CODE POINT (character) position of the first occurrence, matching s[i]'s code point indexing; -1 when absent
`
  'str.index-from': `
searches s for the substring target starting at from (a code point position)
Returns the CODE POINT (character) position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
`
  'str.last-index': `
reverse search for the last occurrence of a substring (last-index is an alias of rindex)
target str: the substring to look for
returns pos i64: the code point (character) position found, or -1
`
  'str.len': `
returns the number of CHARACTERS (code points) in the string
Matches s[i]'s code point indexing; for a byte count use s.len-bytes()
`
  'str.lines': `
splits on newlines
Supports \n and \r\n
returns out []str: the list of lines (a trailing newline produces no empty line)
`
  'str.new': `
str.new: builds a str from a char slice (a module-level constructor)
A compile-time builtin (ForwardFunc: chars-new): []char is converted byte by byte into bytes and the length is set in one go, with no per-byte .no loop. str is a builtin type whose bytes and length cannot be manipulated from user code, so the whole conversion is emitted as IR by the compiler.
`
  'str.repeat': `
repeats the string times times
returns a new string
`
  'str.replace-char': `
replaces every old-ch in s with new-ch (UTF-8 code points)
returns the resulting string
`
  'str.replace-n': `
replacement with a bounded count
old str: the old substring
new str: the new substring
n i64: the maximum number of replacements; n <= 0 replaces nothing
returns val str: the resulting string
`
  'str.replace-str': `
replaces every occurrence of the substring old in s with new
old str: the substring to replace
new str: the replacement
returns val str: the resulting string
When old is the empty string, returns a copy of the original string unchanged
`
  'str.reverse': `
reverses the string
Writes the reversed s into val
`
  'str.rfind-byte-from': `
the internal byte-level reverse search
Returns the BYTE offset of the last occurrence of target (>= from-byte), or -1 when absent
`
  'str.rindex': `
reverse search for the last occurrence of a substring
target str: the substring to look for
returns pos i64: the code point (character) position found, or -1
`
  'str.rindex-from': `
reverse search for a substring starting at from (a code point position)
Same semantics as Python's rfind(target, from): searches [from, .len) for the last occurrence of target
target str: the substring to look for
from i64: the starting position (inclusive lower bound, in code points)
returns pos i64: the code point (character) position found, or -1
`
  'str.set-byte': 'Returns ?bool (it can fail): nil when out of range (i < 0 or i >= the physical capacity cap), and the write is SKIPPED; some(true) on success. The bound is %str-long\'s cap (physical capacity) rather than len, so the "fill the buffer, then set len" construction still works. A caller may discard the ?bool as a bare statement (optunuse only tracks bound names).'
  'str.slice': `
takes the substring [start, end), with indices as CHARACTER (code point) positions
Matches s[i]'s code point indexing; to slice by byte offset use s.slice-bytes()
`
  'str.slice-bytes': `
takes the substring [start, end) by BYTE offsets (used internally at the byte level)
Matches s.byte(i)'s semantics; used internally when std's split/replace and friends slice by bytes
