  'txt.at': `
[deprecated] txt.at: character (code point) index read — use t[idx] instead (native indexing, returns ?char)
txt.at: gets the code point value at a character position (for a byte offset use t.byte(i))
returns b ?byte: nil when out of range; a code point beyond the byte range is truncated
`
  'txt.byte': `
returns the raw byte value at BYTE offset i (a raw byte read)
Different from txt[i]'s code point indexing; for code that builds the buffer byte by byte
Returns ?byte: nil when out of range (i < 0 or i >= len-bytes()), and the caller must handle the option
Handled by the compiler as a builtin (#{buildin}): codegen intercepts it and expands a bounds check plus GEP+load, with
no std function body; the signature is registered in src/builtin/str.go's BuiltinMethodList
(Intercepted, sharing one entry with str.byte through the bare-name fallback)
`
  'txt.clear': 'empties the text (len=0)'
  'txt.compare': `
lexicographic comparison
returns -1 (a<b), 0 (equal), 1 (a>b)
`
  'txt.contains': 'whether it contains the substring'
  'txt.copy': `
copies a txt
⚠️ DEPRECATED: assignment `a = b` already has copy semantics — txt is a value type (a 256-byte inline
buffer on the stack, data[255] + len), so assignment copies byte by byte. Use plain assignment instead; this is kept for compatibility only.
`
  'txt.count': 'counts the code points'
  'txt.decode-cp': `
decodes one UTF-8 code point starting at position i
returns cp = the code point value, clen = the sequence length in bytes (1-4)
`
  'txt.empty': 'whether it is empty'
  'txt.ends-with': 'whether it ends with the given suffix'
  'txt.eq': `
compares two txt values for equality
⚠️ rewritten 2026-09-21. The old implementation used a single `->` pipeline (`.byte(k) - b.byte(k); c != 0 -> ok = 0;
k == an - 1 -> ok == 0 -> eq = false; k = k + 1`) that only made up its mind at the LAST byte,
so `t('hello').eq('world')` — same length, different content — always returned true.
The `neq:` line in tests/txt.no had been printing 1 all along and was frozen into the golden baseline.
txt.compare already compares byte by byte and branches correctly on differing lengths, so it can simply be reused.
`
  'txt.eq-full': 'exact equality of two txt values (length plus byte-by-byte)'
  'txt.fields': 'splits on whitespace into []txt (consecutive whitespace counts as a single separator)'
  'txt.fill': 'fills the whole data area with a byte value'
  'txt.from-bytes': `
creates a txt from a []byte (truncating to 255 bytes)
returns a txt, truncated when the []byte is longer than 255 bytes
`
  'txt.from-chars': `
builds a new txt from a char slice (written code point by code point, UTF-8 encoded; anything past 255 bytes is dropped)
⚠️ you cannot write code points one at a time with `val[i]: { ok -> it = cs[i] }`: D9's place-match gates on a
READ, and reading `val[0]` on a fresh txt with len=0 goes out of range and returns nil, so the ok arm never runs and
the whole function returns an empty string (txt.from-chars silently lost data because of this).
txt.new (ForwardFunc: chars-new) exists precisely for this: it writes all code points at once and sets the byte length.
`
  'txt.from-hex': `
decodes hexadecimal text into a txt
returns Option: ok(txt) / nil (empty) / err('msg')
`
  'txt.from-str': `
creates a txt from a str (truncating to 255 bytes)
returns a txt, truncated when the str is longer than 255 bytes
`
  'txt.index': 'finds the position of a substring (starting from 0)'
  'txt.index-from': `
searches for a substring starting at from
Returns the position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
`
  'txt.join': `
[]txt.join: joins a txt slice with a separator (truncating past 255 bytes)
sep txt: the separator
returns out txt: the joined result
`
  'txt.last-index': 'an alias of rindex'
  'txt.len': 'returns the number of characters (code points), aligned with str.len; for the byte length use txt.len-bytes()'
  'txt.lines': 'splits on newlines into []txt (supports \\n and \\r\\n)'
  'txt.new': `
txt.new: builds a txt from a char slice (a module-level constructor returning a concrete txt)
A compile-time builtin (ForwardFunc: chars-new): []char is converted byte by byte into bytes and the length is written in one go,
with no per-byte .no loop. txt is a builtin type whose bytes and length cannot be manipulated from user code, so the whole conversion is emitted as IR by the compiler.
Anything past 255 bytes is dropped automatically (the length byte is clamped to 255).
`
  'txt.repeat': `
repeats the content times times (capped at 255 bytes)
returns a new txt
`
  'txt.replace-char': `
replaces every old-ch with new-ch (UTF-8 code points)
returns a new txt (truncated past 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.replace-n': `
replacement with a bounded count
old txt, new txt, n i64: the maximum number of replacements (n<=0 replaces nothing)
`
  'txt.replace-str': `
replaces every occurrence of the substring old with new
returns a new txt (truncated past 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.reverse': 'reverses the text content'
  'txt.rindex': `
reverse search for the last occurrence of a substring
returns the position, or -1 when absent
`
  'txt.rindex-from': 'reverse search for a substring starting at from (returns the last occurrence at position >= from)'
  'txt.set-byte': `
Returns ?bool (it can fail): nil when out of range (i < 0 or i >= the 255-byte physical capacity), and the write is SKIPPED;
some(true) on success. The bound is the physical capacity (a txt is a fixed 255-byte buffer) rather than
len, so the "fill the buffer, then set len" construction (fill / from-str / to-hex / join) still works.
A caller may discard the ?bool as a bare statement (optunuse only covers bound names).
`
