  'vec.at': `
[]t.at: safe indexed access; nil when out of range
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
`
  'vec.clone': '[]t.clone: copies the slice'
  'vec.contains': `
[]t.contains: reports whether the value val is present
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz, or the compiler emits a wrong loop condition i < 0 and the loop never runs
`
  'vec.eq': `
[]t.eq: compares two slices for equality
returns true = equal, false = not equal
`
  'vec.extend': `
[]t.extend: appends every element of another slice
Note: the push logic is inlined to work around a pre-existing LLVM bug in calling .push() from inside a method.
The caller must ensure there is enough capacity.
`
  'vec.fill': '[]t.fill: fills with val'
  'vec.first': `
[]t.first: the first element; an empty slice returns nil
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
`
  'vec.index-of': `
[]t.index-of: returns the index of a value, or -1
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz
`
  'vec.insert': `
[]t.insert: inserts val at position i
i >= .len() behaves like push
Note: the push logic is inlined (.len = cur + 1; .[cur] = val) to work around a pre-existing LLVM bug
(_LB__RB_t.push undefined) triggered by calling .push() inside a method. The caller must ensure there is enough capacity.
`
  'vec.last': `
[]t.last: the last element; an empty slice returns nil
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
`
  'vec.len': '[]t.len: returns the slice length'
  'vec.max': `
[]t.max: the maximum value; an empty slice returns nil
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz
`
  'vec.min': `
[]t.min: the minimum value; an empty slice returns nil
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz
`
  'vec.pop': `
[]t.pop: pops the element at the end
An empty slice returns nil
`
  'vec.remove': `
[]t.remove: removes the element at position i and returns it
Out of range returns nil
`
  'vec.reverse': '[]t.reverse: reverses in place'
  'vec.sum': `
[]t.sum: the sum
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz
`
  'vec.swap': `
[]t.swap: swaps the elements at two positions
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
`
  'vec.to-arr': '[]t.to-arr: a slice method that converts to an [n]t array'
  'vec.to-str': `
[]t.to-str: the string form
Formatted like '[1, 2, 3]'
Elements are stringified by calling each one's own .to-str() (see number.no for the concrete types)
`
  'vec.truncate': '[]t.truncate: truncates to cnt elements; cnt < 0 is treated as 0, and cnt >= .len does nothing'
  'vec.vec-clear': `
[]t.clear: empties the slice (sets the length to 0 in place)
Does not free the heap and does not shrink the capacity, so the data pointer is unchanged
Only a write with new content of non-zero length goes through the growth logic
`
  'vec.vec-push': `
[]t.push: appends an element at the end (growing automatically)
Growth policy:
cap == 0 -> new-cap = 4
cap < 1024 -> new-cap = cap * 2
cap >= 1024 -> new-cap = cap * 5 / 4
`
