  'yaml.yaml-parser.skip-doc-preamble': 'Skips blank lines, comments, and directive lines'
  'yaml.yaml-parser.skip-flow-ws': 'Flow-context whitespace: inline spaces, newlines, and comments'
  'yaml.yaml-parser.skip-spaces': 'Skips inline spaces and tabs'
  'yaml.yaml-parser.skip-to-doc-boundary': 'Skips the rest of the current document, stopping at the next --- or the end of input'
  'yaml.yaml-parser.skip-to-eol': 'Skips trailing whitespace and line comments (the newline is not consumed)'
  'yaml.yaml-plain-safe': 'Whether the text can be emitted verbatim as a plain scalar'
  'yaml.yaml-quote': 'Emits a double-quoted YAML scalar'
  'yaml.yaml-resolve-plain': 'Infers the plain scalar\'s kind using the core schema'
  'yaml.yaml-scalar-repr': 'The YAML representation of a scalar'
  'yaml.yaml-scan-number': `
Tries to parse the text as an integer or a float
Returns kind = YAML-KIND-INT, YAML-KIND-FLOAT, or YAML-KIND-STR (not a number)
`
  'yaml.yaml-spaces': 'Produces n spaces'
  'yaml.yaml-str-at': `
Why this helper exists: `arr[i]` (with arr a []str) returns option<str> by default, and the
MIR backend produces illegal IR when an option<str> is used as a call argument or method
receiver (%option_str does not match %str-long). String concatenation unwraps the option
automatically, so the element is fetched with `out = out - a[i]`, which both unwraps it and
satisfies the `no vet` bounds check.
`
  'yaml.yaml-tag-name': 'Normalises a tag name (!!str and tag:yaml.org,2002:str both become str)'
  'yaml.yaml-to-f64': 'Converts text to f64 (nil means failure)'
  'yaml.yaml-to-i64': 'Converts text to i64; -1 on failure'
  'yaml.yaml-unescape-double': 'Handles double-quoted escape sequences'
